    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if my LDL is high, how does it actually affect my arteries? like, what is the biological process of it actually causing a problem?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When LDL levels are high, cholesterol molecules are deposited into the wall of your arteries, forming plaques. Over time, this accumulation can lead to the narrowing and hardening of the arteries, reducing blood flow to your heart, brain, and other vital organs. The plaque can also rupture, triggering inflammation and the formation of blood clots, which can cause a heart attack or stroke.",
      "source": "target",
